    "body": "<!-- pr-reviewer: 59a0f6f0083ff7460af48e125aa061ca2b28fb61 -->\n**Independent review (v0.14.0 cut), static.** I read the diff, `fanuc-lathe.cps` (`onThreadCycle` / `g76Why`), `cnc_thread.rs`, `turning/thread.rs::attach_cycle` and the golden. Verdict: **clean, no blocking items.**\n\n### Word values against the record (golden: M12x2 external, 0.4 mm rungs)\n- Block 1, `G76 P010000 Q400 R0`: m=01 (1 + spring 0), r=00 (no pull-out chamfer), a=00 (radial). `Q` = dmin as a radius in whole microns (0.4 -> 400). `R` = finish allowance in mm. Correct.\n- Block 2, `G76 X9.6 Z14 P1200 Q400 F2`: X = minor diameter in diameter mode. Z = end. No `R` taper (straight thread). `P` = height as a radius in microns, (12 - 9.6)/2 = 1.2 -> 1200. `Q` = first depth in microns. `F` = lead in mm/rev. Correct. The control's crest, X + 2k = 12.0, matches the record's major diameter.\n- Depth sequence: with dmin = first depth, the control's sqrt(n) increments Δd(√n - √(n-1)) all clamp to dmin, so it cuts 0.4 / 0.8 / 1.2 like the kernel's rungs, with the last rung clamped to k. m=1 adds one full-depth pass at the verified root, as RECOMMENDED says.\n- The start point is the kernel's own `rho_clear` (X12.1, Z8), the same clearance the G32 passes retract to. The out-rapid (`G00 X12.1 Z24`) stays outside the span. The G32 default posts byte-identical with or without the record (pinned).\n- Infeed angle 0 matches the kernel's radial plunge. G97/G99 are in force from the preamble. The kernel is single-start (lead = pitch), so F is right.\n- Refusals: a missing or out-of-range record, internal thread, start inside the crest, end off the minor diameter, no length or lead. Each `refuse()` is a counted message, so the gate blocks it and nothing silently posts as G32. G76 chosen with passes but no record is refused in `threadHalfTurn`.\n\n### Non-blocking\n- `R` in block 1 is printed by `fu`, which trims to an integer (`R1` for 1 mm). On a control without calculator-type decimal input, a word without a decimal point reads in least-input increments, so `R1` would mean 1 micron. Today it is always `R0`, so this is moot until a non-zero finish allowance is generated. Consider forcing the decimal point there (`R1.`).\n- The radial clearance at the cycle start is 0.05 mm over the crest. The G76 return rapids run along Z at that X. G32 does the same, so this does not regress, but it is tight on a real machine (a kernel `rho_clear` question).\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
